WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:04.397
<v Examiner>Multiple alleles, blood groups, and X-linked genes.

2
00:00:04.847 --> 00:00:09.346
<v Examiner>Briefly explain multiple allele inheritance and sex-linked inheritance,

3
00:00:09.346 --> 00:00:11.311
<v Examiner>using the right genetics terms.

4
00:00:11.761 --> 00:00:16.186
Two more of the inheritance patterns on her checklist: multiple alleles,

5
00:00:16.186 --> 00:00:17.354
and X-linked genes.

6
00:00:17.354 --> 00:00:22.641
Her quizzes turn both into scenario questions, so it's worth having the crosses ready.

7
00:00:23.091 --> 00:00:27.379
The hook: blood groups have three alleles, and a boy's X has no partner.

8
00:00:27.829 --> 00:00:32.740
Multiple allele inheritance: a trait controlled by more than two alleles.

9
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.095
Her example is the ABO blood group.

10
00:00:35.095 --> 00:00:39.939
The gene comes in three versions, A, B and O, and you carry two of them,

11
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:41.756
so there are six genotypes.

12
00:00:41.756 --> 00:00:44.313
Genotypes AA and AO both give group A.

13
00:00:44.313 --> 00:00:46.197
BB and BO both give group B.

14
00:00:46.197 --> 00:00:47.408
AB gives group AB.

15
00:00:47.408 --> 00:00:48.821
And OO gives group O.

16
00:00:48.821 --> 00:00:53.194
The A and B alleles are codominant, and both are dominant over O.

17
00:00:53.644 --> 00:00:55.002
The crosses she asks.

18
00:00:55.002 --> 00:01:00.434
AO crossed with BO gives all four groups, A, B, AB and O, twenty-five per cent each,

19
00:01:00.434 --> 00:01:03.732
which is how one family can have four blood groups.

20
00:01:03.732 --> 00:01:06.253
An O child needs an O from each parent.

21
00:01:06.253 --> 00:01:11.038
A man who is OO, whose children are all AO, must have a partner who is AA.

22
00:01:11.038 --> 00:01:13.625
And a man who is homozygous for group A,

23
00:01:13.625 --> 00:01:17.699
crossed with a woman who is heterozygous for group B, AA by BO,

24
00:01:17.699 --> 00:01:20.673
gives fifty per cent AB and fifty per cent AO.

25
00:01:21.123 --> 00:01:25.610
The trap she sets: polygenic is not the same as multiple alleles.

26
00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.474
Polygenic means many genes.

27
00:01:27.474 --> 00:01:31.615
Multiple alleles means one gene with more than two versions.

28
00:01:31.615 --> 00:01:35.067
Her true or false says blood groups are polygenic.

29
00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:35.481
False.

30
00:01:36.931 --> 00:01:39.197
X-linked, or sex-linked, inheritance.

31
00:01:39.197 --> 00:01:41.280
The gene sits on the X chromosome.

32
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.568
A male has one X and a Y, and the Y has no matching allele to mask it.

33
00:01:45.568 --> 00:01:49.610
So one copy of a recessive X-linked allele always shows in a male,

34
00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.142
while a female needs two.

35
00:01:51.142 --> 00:01:56.655
That's why males inherit more sex-linked conditions, and why only females can be carriers.

36
00:01:56.655 --> 00:02:01.004
Her drop-down: males cannot be carriers, because they only carry one X.

37
00:02:01.004 --> 00:02:04.067
Her examples are colour blindness and haemophilia.

38
00:02:04.517 --> 00:02:05.433
Her two crosses.

39
00:02:05.433 --> 00:02:10.184
A carrier mother, X big B, X little b, and a father with normal vision, X big B, Y:

40
00:02:10.184 --> 00:02:13.562
each son has a fifty per cent chance of being colour blind,

41
00:02:13.562 --> 00:02:17.684
and none of the daughters will be, though half of them will be carriers.

42
00:02:17.684 --> 00:02:22.722
A colour-blind father, X little b, Y, and a mother with normal vision, X big B, X big B:

43
00:02:22.722 --> 00:02:26.043
none of the sons are colour blind, because they get his Y,

44
00:02:26.043 --> 00:02:29.306
and all of the daughters are carriers with normal vision.

45
00:02:29.756 --> 00:02:30.364
<v Examiner>Once more.

46
00:02:30.364 --> 00:02:32.615
<v Examiner>Multiple alleles, and X-linked genes.

47
00:02:35.065 --> 00:02:40.375
Multiple alleles: one trait with more than two alleles, like ABO blood: three alleles,

48
00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:42.043
six genotypes, four groups.

49
00:02:42.043 --> 00:02:45.748
The A and B alleles are codominant and both dominant over O,

50
00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:50.071
so AO crossed with BO gives A, B, AB and O, twenty-five per cent each.

51
00:02:50.071 --> 00:02:51.244
It isn't polygenic.

52
00:02:51.244 --> 00:02:55.196
X-linked: the gene is on the X and the Y has no matching allele,

53
00:02:55.196 --> 00:03:00.568
so one copy shows in a male while a female needs two, and only females can be carriers.

54
00:03:00.568 --> 00:03:05.447
A carrier mother and a normal father: half the sons affected, and no daughters.

55
00:03:05.447 --> 00:03:10.819
An affected father and a normal mother: no affected sons, and every daughter a carrier.
